      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The historic King of Prussia Inn, built in 1719, earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places and was spared by building lanes around it.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Prussia%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "King of Prussia hosts the Nuclear Regulatory Commission Region I headquarters, which oversees 25 nuclear reactors across 16 plants in the Northeast.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Prussia%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area once served as a quiet crossroads, even hosting General Washington’s winter camp at Valley Forge during 1777‑78.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Prussia%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Originally an auto‑dependent suburb, King of Prussia shifted toward a higher‑density grid that welcomes pedestrians, bicyclists, and transit users.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
